1
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:47,000
It was a typical evening in southeastern Nebraska.

2
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,460
Folks went about their work and their pleasure as usual.

3
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:54,740
Just another evening, the same as the night before.

4
00:00:54,740 --> 00:00:58,359
So far as they could tell, for example, there was nothing different about the source of

5
00:00:58,359 --> 00:01:03,740
the electricity that lighted their living rooms, that cooked their dinners, that powered

6
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:10,680
their television sets, that pumped their gasoline, that performed 101 other duties to make their

7
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:22,840
work more efficient, their lives more enjoyable.

8
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:27,599
But there was a difference in the source of that electricity, a significant difference.

9
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:32,820
For the first time in Nebraska, that electricity was being produced by nuclear energy.

10
00:01:32,820 --> 00:01:38,480
The nuclear power facility at Hallam, Nebraska, a 240,000 thermal kilowatt sodium graphite

11
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:44,680
reactor, capable of producing 75,000 kilowatts of electricity, was on the line.

12
00:01:57,600 --> 00:02:17,900
Located on a 600-acre site 20 miles from Nebraska's capital city of Lincoln, the Hallam Nuclear

13
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:22,860
Power Facility looms up in startling contrast to the surrounding farmlands.

14
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:28,660
The complex of buildings includes a reactor wing, turbine generator wing with conventional

15
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:39,000
electric generating facilities, an administration wing, cooling tower, waste storage building,

16
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,920
and substation.

17
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:48,400
Overall size of the reactor wing is roughly 116 feet by 275 feet.

18
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:53,300
The plant was designed with prime consideration for safety, in the handling of radioactive

19
00:02:53,300 --> 00:03:00,800
waste materials, in the unlikely event of an accident.

20
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:05,780
The Hallam Nuclear Facility was built jointly by the Atomic Energy Commission and Consumers

21
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:11,460
Public Power District of Nebraska, and will be operated by consumers under the AEC's Power

22
00:03:11,460 --> 00:03:14,900
Demonstration Reactor Program.

23
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:19,319
One objective of this program is to advance the time when the cost of nuclear power will

24
00:03:19,319 --> 00:03:23,700
be reduced to levels competitive with power from conventional fuels.

25
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:28,099
Another important objective is to advance the state of nuclear reactor technology in

26
00:03:28,099 --> 00:03:29,460
general.

27
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:33,980
The specific objective of the Hellam project is to demonstrate the economic and technical

28
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:39,840
practicality of a central station power plant using a sodium graphite reactor.

29
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:44,040
This will require operation of the plant for the primary purpose of producing the maximum

30
00:03:44,039 --> 00:03:49,459
amount of electric energy. It is anticipated that operation of the Hallam facility will

31
00:03:49,459 --> 00:03:53,519
provide knowledge and experience that will lead to the realization of competitive nuclear

32
00:03:53,519 --> 00:04:02,159
power in subsequent reactor plants. The Hallam facility is one of a family of reactors which

33
00:04:02,159 --> 00:04:06,819
have demonstrated the technical advantages of liquid metal coolant. The others include

34
00:04:06,819 --> 00:04:12,799
the experimental breeder reactor one, which in 1951 produced the first electricity generated

35
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:16,259
with nuclear energy.

36
00:04:16,259 --> 00:04:20,540
The Experimental Breeder Reactor II.

37
00:04:20,540 --> 00:04:25,139
The privately financed Enrico Fermi Fast Breeder Reactor.

38
00:04:25,139 --> 00:04:28,139
And the Sodium Reactor Experiment.

39
00:04:28,139 --> 00:04:33,840
The SRE, in which the technical feasibility of the sodium graphite concept was demonstrated,

40
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:39,480
was built and operated for the AEC near Los Angeles, California by Atomics International,

41
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:43,160
a division of North American Aviation Incorporated.

42
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:46,360
As a result of his experience with the sodium reactor experiment,

43
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:50,340
Atomics International was assigned the overall technical responsibility

44
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:54,580
for the design of the Hallam facility.

45
00:04:54,580 --> 00:04:58,860
Construction of the Hallam facility was authorized in 1957.

46
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:03,540
The plant site was chosen on the basis of Nebraska's population and power load center

47
00:05:03,540 --> 00:05:09,400
and the availability of land, water and transportation facilities.

48
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:16,640
Groundbreaking ceremonies on a gently rolling hill typical of the surrounding terrain were held on June 28, 1958.

49
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:26,580
In a conventional steam power plant, heat released by the combustion of fossil fuels, oil, natural gas, or coal,

50
00:05:27,180 --> 00:05:32,140
is applied to high-pressure boilers to produce the steam that turns the turbine generator.

51
00:05:32,139 --> 00:05:39,139
In the Hellum nuclear power facility, atomic fuel is substituted for the fossil fuel in the production of steam.

52
00:05:39,139 --> 00:05:49,139
At Hellum, conventional fossil fuel facilities constructed by Consumers Public Power District have been integrated with the nuclear facility.

53
00:05:49,139 --> 00:05:55,139
The conventional boiler and auxiliaries with the turbine generator now operated as part of the nuclear facility

54
00:05:55,139 --> 00:06:01,139
were installed by consumers to supply generating capacity needed in advance of the nuclear facility's completion date

55
00:06:01,139 --> 00:06:05,599
date and during the period of reactor test operation.

56
00:06:05,599 --> 00:06:10,099
The conventional facility was designed to match the reactor steam conditions of 800

57
00:06:10,099 --> 00:06:15,899
pounds per square inch of pressure and 825 degrees Fahrenheit temperature.

58
00:06:15,899 --> 00:06:21,560
The two facilities, conventional and nuclear, have centralized control so integrated that

59
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:27,180
the turbine generator can be operated automatically using either source of steam.

60
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:31,500
completion of the reactor test operation it is planned to release the fossil fuel

61
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:35,280
boiler from the system for use with a second generating unit which may be

62
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:41,639
built later on the site the heart of the helm nuclear facility is the reactor

63
00:06:41,639 --> 00:06:46,899
core the fuel is a metallic uranium element contained in stainless steel

64
00:06:46,899 --> 00:06:52,120
tubes which are suspended in clusters within the core moderator elements made

65
00:06:52,120 --> 00:06:58,840
graphite blocks surround the fuel elements. Liquid sodium serves as the coolant and heat transfer

66
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:04,680
medium because of its high boiling point and excellent heat transfer properties. Starting at

67
00:07:04,680 --> 00:07:10,120
the bottom of the reactor vessel, the sodium flows up past the hot fuel elements to an upper pool

68
00:07:10,840 --> 00:07:16,040
and then out of the vessel at the top at a temperature of approximately 950 degrees

69
00:07:16,040 --> 00:07:22,040
Fahrenheit. The primary sodium coolant becomes radioactive during its passage through the

70
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:28,400
core. This radioactive sodium is pumped through three identical primary loops to intermediate

71
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:33,939
heat exchangers and returned to the reactor vessel at approximately 600 degrees Fahrenheit

72
00:07:33,939 --> 00:07:39,879
to repeat the cycle. The heat carried by the sodium in the primary loops is transferred

73
00:07:39,879 --> 00:07:46,140
by the intermediate heat exchangers to the three secondary sodium loops which are non-radioactive.

74
00:07:46,139 --> 00:07:51,699
The heat from the secondary non-radioactive loops is utilized in three steam generators,

75
00:07:51,699 --> 00:07:56,459
producing the steam that turns the conventional turbine generator.

76
00:07:56,459 --> 00:08:01,560
With installation of the reactor core cavity liner scheduled as the first major objective,

77
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:06,919
full-scale construction activities at the plant site began early in 1959.

78
00:08:06,919 --> 00:08:11,680
The reactor building excavation was 80 feet deep at its deepest point, the point where

79
00:08:11,680 --> 00:08:14,199
the maintenance cell is installed.

80
00:08:14,199 --> 00:08:18,519
Reinforced concrete floors were built up to a thickness of four feet.

81
00:08:18,519 --> 00:08:25,899
Poured, reinforced concrete walls are even thicker, up to six feet in some places.

82
00:08:25,899 --> 00:08:32,080
The stainless steel reactor core tank, 19 feet in diameter by 33 feet in depth, is surrounded

83
00:08:32,080 --> 00:08:45,980
by a thermal shield, an outer tank, a layer of thermal insulation, and the reactor cavity

84
00:08:45,980 --> 00:08:49,920
liner, which is made of half-inch carbon steel.

85
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:54,879
Steel pipe welded to the outer surface of the reactor cavity liner provides a means

86
00:08:54,879 --> 00:08:59,759
of circulating demineralized water to remove heat from the surrounding concrete.

87
00:08:59,759 --> 00:09:03,860
A pipe gallery accommodates sodium inlets and outlets to the core tank.

88
00:09:04,840 --> 00:09:08,480
The reactor cavity liner is surrounded by reinforced concrete,

89
00:09:08,899 --> 00:09:12,939
which provides a structural support as well as a radiation and thermal shield.

90
00:09:13,919 --> 00:09:19,120
The upper six and one-third feet of the adjacent wall is made of high-density concrete.

91
00:09:20,639 --> 00:09:24,840
In the bottom of the cavity liner, individual columns two and a half feet high

92
00:09:24,840 --> 00:09:29,720
and one and one-half feet in diameter were installed to provide support for the outer vessel.

93
00:09:29,759 --> 00:09:38,759
Fabricated in Philadelphia, the reactor core vessel and its outer tank were transported by waterway to Plattsmouth, Nebraska.

94
00:09:38,759 --> 00:09:46,759
To accommodate the overland movement of the huge vessels, it was necessary to widen and strengthen bridges and move utility lines.

95
00:09:46,759 --> 00:09:54,759
The outer vessel shown in this model is twenty and two-thirds feet in diameter and thirty-six feet high,

96
00:09:54,759 --> 00:09:58,759
and was fabricated from half inch thick low alloy steel.

97
00:09:58,759 --> 00:10:06,759
The outer wall of the outer vessel is covered by a 12-inch layer of thermal insulation, made up of individual blocks of asbestos fiber.

98
00:10:07,759 --> 00:10:12,759
The insulation serves to hold the heat within the reactor, thus adding to overall efficiency.

99
00:10:16,759 --> 00:10:19,759
The insulation also protects the surrounding concrete from high temperatures.

100
00:10:19,759 --> 00:10:29,600
high temperatures. The bottom of the outer tank supports the core tank as well as the

101
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:35,539
thermal shield. The thermal shield, which surrounds the core tank, was made of two concentric

102
00:10:35,539 --> 00:10:41,139
rings cast from low carbon steel. Their function is to absorb most of the heat due to gamma

103
00:10:41,139 --> 00:10:49,639
rays and neutrons. Each cylinder is formed by a series of ten adjacent vertical curved

104
00:10:49,639 --> 00:10:54,439
which rest on the bottom of the reactor outer tank and are 33 and 1 3rd

105
00:10:54,439 --> 00:11:01,580
feet high the reactor vessel is made of stainless steel 3 quarters of an inch

106
00:11:01,580 --> 00:11:05,840
thick except for the base and the areas of the inlet and outlet sodium nozzles

107
00:11:05,840 --> 00:11:11,360
which are 2 inches thick the bottom was formed cold and the sides were built in

108
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:19,340
successive ring sections each welded in place a dome like containment shell

109
00:11:19,340 --> 00:11:23,120
used with some nuclear power plants is not required for the Hallam reactor

110
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:31,820
because the sodium coolant is not under pressure. The reactor vessel and the

111
00:11:31,820 --> 00:11:36,820
primary sodium system are surrounded by a series of steel-lined concrete cells.

112
00:11:36,820 --> 00:11:41,600
These nitrogen-filled cells would prevent the escape of radioactive sodium

113
00:11:41,600 --> 00:11:47,220
vapor or fission product gases into the atmosphere and radioactive liquids into

114
00:11:47,220 --> 00:11:54,180
surrounding subsoil. Three main sodium inlet nozzles are located 28 inches above the base

115
00:11:54,740 --> 00:12:01,779
and three sodium outlet nozzles leave the vessel 23 feet above the base. At the top a stainless

116
00:12:01,779 --> 00:12:07,540
steel bellows assembly seals the reactor vessel to the upper cavity liner to prevent sodium vapors

117
00:12:07,540 --> 00:12:15,139
and the helium cover gas from escaping. Within this vessel liquid sodium flows through the core

118
00:12:15,139 --> 00:12:18,840
at the rate of 21,600 gallons per minute.

119
00:12:18,939 --> 00:12:24,840
A stainless steel grid plate is supported 42 inches above the bottom of the core tank by individual columns

120
00:12:24,939 --> 00:12:26,639
and a support ring.

121
00:12:26,740 --> 00:12:33,639
The grid precisely locates the moderator elements with relation to the fuel elements.

122
00:12:33,740 --> 00:12:39,539
The moderator elements consist of logs of graphite sealed in stainless steel cans.

123
00:12:39,639 --> 00:12:42,840
The function of the moderator is to slow down neutrons released

124
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:47,899
during atomic fission to increase the probability that the neutrons will initiate further fission

125
00:12:47,899 --> 00:12:50,780
and thus sustain the controlled chain reaction.

126
00:12:52,680 --> 00:12:57,840
The graphite logs, six to a can, were machined into a hexagonal shape.

127
00:12:59,820 --> 00:13:04,860
The stainless steel sheath material forming the sides of the can is 16 one-thousandths

128
00:13:04,860 --> 00:13:05,519
of an inch thick.

129
00:13:06,320 --> 00:13:09,519
The sheath is made up of two form panels welded lengthwise.

130
00:13:09,519 --> 00:13:15,419
The base of the element is half inch thick stainless steel casting, which includes the

131
00:13:15,419 --> 00:13:21,939
six inch high pedestal supporting the moderator element on the bottom grid plate.

132
00:13:21,939 --> 00:13:26,019
Spacers maintain a 16-100 inch clearance between moderator elements.

133
00:13:26,019 --> 00:13:30,639
In addition, core clamps are provided on the internal vessel wall to hold the moderator

134
00:13:30,639 --> 00:13:32,620
elements in place.

135
00:13:32,620 --> 00:13:36,419
The clearance between the elements permits the circulation of small amounts of sodium

136
00:13:36,420 --> 00:13:39,640
to cool the graphite.

137
00:13:39,640 --> 00:13:45,240
To avoid contamination, components were continually cleaned during installation to remove lint

138
00:13:45,240 --> 00:13:48,880
or other foreign matter.

139
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:55,020
With the moderator assembly in place, the huge top shield, weighing about 300 tons,

140
00:13:55,020 --> 00:13:57,500
was placed over the tank.

141
00:13:57,500 --> 00:14:03,060
Six feet thick, the shield is filled with lead and dense concrete and provides radiation

142
00:14:03,060 --> 00:14:06,860
and thermal shielding directly above the reactor core.

143
00:14:06,860 --> 00:14:11,700
The shield can be rotated and has removable plugs which extend completely through the

144
00:14:11,700 --> 00:14:12,700
shield.

145
00:14:12,700 --> 00:14:20,960
These plugs permit access for the installation or removal of fuel and moderator elements.

146
00:14:20,960 --> 00:14:27,020
Each fuel element is made up of individual rods of 3.6% enriched uranium molybdenum alloy.

147
00:14:27,020 --> 00:14:31,760
The rods are approximately six-tenths of an inch in diameter and 13 and one-half feet

148
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:32,760
long.

149
00:14:32,759 --> 00:14:38,139
They were assembled by inserting 3-inch to 12-inch length uranium slugs into the stainless steel jacket.

150
00:14:39,039 --> 00:14:45,360
These uranium slugs were cast to size, and after cleaning, the materials were subjected to tests to assure quality.

151
00:14:46,200 --> 00:14:54,360
The slugs were then cut to usable lengths, and after again cleaning the material, it was ready for insertion into the stainless steel container.

152
00:14:55,120 --> 00:15:01,039
The space between the inner wall of the jacket and the fuel slugs was then filled with sodium

153
00:15:01,039 --> 00:15:05,080
to improve the transfer of heat from the uranium fuel to the jacket.

154
00:15:05,980 --> 00:15:10,360
After quality control tests were completed to ensure the integrity of the assembly,

155
00:15:11,039 --> 00:15:13,820
the stainless steel jacket was capped, sealed, and welded.

156
00:15:14,879 --> 00:15:19,940
Eighteen of these rods were then assembled in a bundle, supported at the top and bottom by a bracket,

157
00:15:19,940 --> 00:15:23,899
and the assembly then inserted into a zirconium process tube.

158
00:15:24,360 --> 00:15:32,360
At the upper end of the process tube there is a variable orifice which is used to control the amount of sodium flowing over the fuel element.

159
00:15:32,360 --> 00:15:37,360
And in this way regulates the operating temperature of the fuel element.

160
00:15:37,360 --> 00:15:45,360
A continuing program of testing assured that the work was being conducted to meet the rigorous specifications.

161
00:15:45,360 --> 00:15:55,360
Reactor control is maintained by 19 combination regulating and safety rods inserted down into the reactor core.

162
00:15:55,360 --> 00:16:01,360
Each rod is made up of rare earth oxides which absorb neutrons.

163
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:09,360
Each rod is positioned within the core by a motor-driven gear reduction unit mounted on a carriage over the reactor core

164
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:13,279
is held to the drive motor unit by an electromagnet.

165
00:16:13,279 --> 00:16:17,519
Any malfunction would interrupt the power to these electromagnets,

166
00:16:17,519 --> 00:16:21,899
and the rods would then drop into the core to shut down the reactor.

167
00:16:21,899 --> 00:16:26,279
During the shutdown periods, the neutron absorbers stay in the core,

168
00:16:26,279 --> 00:16:30,360
and the disconnected overhead carriage is removed to provide easy access

169
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:32,039
to the face of the upper shield.

170
00:16:33,800 --> 00:16:38,440
The use of an intermediate heat exchanger is required because the sodium becomes radioactive

171
00:16:38,440 --> 00:16:42,800
passing through the reactor core. Each intermediate heat exchanger is made up

172
00:16:42,800 --> 00:16:47,740
of two units operating in parallel. Each unit is of the shell and tube counter

173
00:16:47,740 --> 00:16:52,960
flow design with the primary sodium contained in the tube side. The stainless

174
00:16:52,960 --> 00:16:57,380
steel exchangers are mounted vertically in the heat exchanger cell. Thermal

175
00:16:57,380 --> 00:17:01,600
expansion is provided by a bellows mounted in the straight shell section

176
00:17:01,600 --> 00:17:06,080
and permits solid construction of the tubes containing the radioactive sodium.

177
00:17:06,079 --> 00:17:10,919
In the intermediate heat exchanger, the secondary loop of non-radioactive sodium

178
00:17:10,919 --> 00:17:18,179
is heated by the primary sodium to 895 degrees Fahrenheit. The hot sodium is

179
00:17:18,179 --> 00:17:23,399
pumped by centrifugal pumps to the three steam generators. These pumps are similar

180
00:17:23,399 --> 00:17:29,500
to those used in oil refineries. Each steam generator consists of an

181
00:17:29,500 --> 00:17:34,359
evaporator, moisture eliminator, and super heater. Construction is of the shell and

182
00:17:34,360 --> 00:17:39,960
and tube type using double wall bayonet tubes to prevent leakage between sodium and water systems.

183
00:17:40,600 --> 00:17:47,080
The bayonet tubes utilize helium between the two walls as a monitor for leaks. The evaporator

184
00:17:47,080 --> 00:17:52,760
is a kettle type design with the water carried in the shell. The moisture eliminator delivers

185
00:17:52,760 --> 00:17:59,880
dry steam to the super heater. Steam piping connects each of the three steam generators

186
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:05,800
to the main steam line and the 825 degree fahrenheit 800 pounds per square inch steam

187
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:11,160
is fed to the turbine generator after the steam passes through the turbine it is condensed to

188
00:18:11,160 --> 00:18:16,920
water and returned to the individual steam generators at 325 degrees fahrenheit high

189
00:18:16,920 --> 00:18:20,920
grade superheated steam is produced by the liquid sodium heated generators

190
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:26,440
the turbine generator is basically of conventional hydrogen cool design

191
00:18:26,440 --> 00:18:32,539
line. While rated at 100,000 kilowatts, it will have an output of 75,000 kilowatts in

192
00:18:32,539 --> 00:18:39,220
the initial reactor operation. It was actually put on the line the spring of 1961, operating

193
00:18:39,220 --> 00:18:46,420
with the conventional steam facilities. The substation uses a single transformer to convert

194
00:18:46,420 --> 00:18:53,240
the 13,800 volts of the generator to the 115,000 volt transmission lines, which distribute

195
00:18:53,240 --> 00:18:59,480
power to the eastern two-thirds of Nebraska. Auxiliary transformers supply power to the plant

196
00:18:59,480 --> 00:19:08,039
for internal use. In January 1962, the plant was ready for preliminary testing of nuclear

197
00:19:08,039 --> 00:19:14,279
equipment. It began with a dry critical test. The installation and removal of the fuel elements is

198
00:19:14,279 --> 00:19:18,960
is handled by a fuel handling cask heavily shielded by lead against radiation.

199
00:19:22,259 --> 00:19:25,519
The 55 foot high chamber is supported by a gantry crane.

200
00:19:26,440 --> 00:19:31,460
When a fuel element is to be changed, a fresh element is picked up from the storage area.

201
00:19:31,460 --> 00:19:35,119
The cask is then moved to a position above the reactor.

202
00:19:35,119 --> 00:19:38,759
An indexing mechanism moves the cask to a preset position

203
00:19:38,759 --> 00:19:41,220
precisely in line with the given fuel element.

204
00:19:41,220 --> 00:19:48,420
A pneumatic mechanism forces the lead radiation shield down onto the surface of the loading space.

205
00:19:48,420 --> 00:19:53,180
A gas-tight seal is made.

206
00:19:53,180 --> 00:19:59,579
A pickup device within the cask is lowered to engage the shielding plug of the used fuel element.

207
00:19:59,579 --> 00:20:05,519
The plug and spent fuel element are lifted out of the core as one unit.

208
00:20:05,519 --> 00:20:10,460
The revolving fuel support plate at the top of the cask is then rotated 180 degrees to

209
00:20:10,460 --> 00:20:21,900
position the new fuel element over the empty fuel channel in the core.

210
00:20:21,900 --> 00:20:27,740
The new fuel element and plug are lowered into place.

211
00:20:27,740 --> 00:20:32,319
The cask then carries the used fuel element to a storage cell where the radioactivity

212
00:20:32,319 --> 00:20:35,000
is allowed to decay for a period of 60 to 90 days.

213
00:20:36,299 --> 00:20:39,639
Then it is moved to a cleaning cell where it is steam cleaned and rinsed

214
00:20:39,639 --> 00:20:41,960
to remove any sodium adhering to the outer surface.

215
00:20:43,139 --> 00:20:46,319
From the cleaning cell, the fuel element is moved to the maintenance cell

216
00:20:46,319 --> 00:20:51,159
where the fuel rod bundle is removed and prepared for shipping to a chemical processing center.

217
00:20:53,059 --> 00:20:57,480
The water that has been used in the cleaning cell is transferred from the cleaning cell

218
00:20:57,480 --> 00:21:01,259
to a radioactive liquid waste system where it is held in large tanks.

219
00:21:01,900 --> 00:21:07,500
The contents of these tanks are analyzed for radioactivity, and if there is any significant radioactivity,

220
00:21:07,500 --> 00:21:14,420
the contents are treated to remove the radioactivity, and the residue is then packaged for shipment to waste disposal sites.

221
00:21:15,160 --> 00:21:18,799
If, however, the contents are free of significant radioactivity,

222
00:21:19,180 --> 00:21:22,599
the water is allowed to seep into the ground at the plant leaching field.

223
00:21:23,299 --> 00:21:27,379
This same method is used to analyze any liquid that might be radioactive,

224
00:21:28,019 --> 00:21:29,940
even the water used in the plant laundry.

225
00:21:29,940 --> 00:21:36,940
This conservative program is further supplemented by a continuing program of radiation monitoring in the plant,

226
00:21:36,940 --> 00:21:43,940
as well as the monitoring of the air, soil, plants, and water, which make up the environment of the plant area.

227
00:21:45,940 --> 00:21:50,940
As each system was completed, operating personnel started a program of test operation,

228
00:21:50,940 --> 00:21:56,940
which assured that the equipment would perform satisfactorily under both normal and abnormal conditions.

229
00:21:56,940 --> 00:22:04,940
As an increasing portion of the plant was completed, this testing program was expanded until the entire plant was ready for insertion of fuel.

230
00:22:04,940 --> 00:22:12,940
In January 1962, the reactor was placed in operation for the first time in dry critical tests.

231
00:22:12,940 --> 00:22:16,940
Wet critical low-power tests were started in August 1962.

232
00:22:16,940 --> 00:22:22,940
These low-power tests provided further assurance that the plant was operating satisfactorily.

233
00:22:22,940 --> 00:22:28,120
and a period of about nine months was used to carry the testing program through to its full

234
00:22:28,120 --> 00:22:34,860
power operation. The inherent safety of the plant was proved as the reliability of the control and

235
00:22:34,860 --> 00:22:41,539
safety equipment was determined and verified with each increase in power. This safety program,

236
00:22:41,539 --> 00:22:46,759
which was started with the conception of the plant, included inspection of individual components as

237
00:22:46,759 --> 00:22:51,960
they were being fabricated and was carried through to a detailed safety analysis of the completed

238
00:22:51,960 --> 00:22:58,100
plant. Emphasis on the safety program will continue throughout the period of plant operation.

239
00:23:02,579 --> 00:23:09,799
In 1963, electricity produced by nuclear power began flowing through the substation.

240
00:23:10,860 --> 00:23:14,779
The Hallam Nuclear Power Facility was on the line.
